#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# fresnel_f

#% text
# `fresnel_f(z)` gives the Fresnel auxiliary function `f(z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `f(z)` is `(1/2 - fresnel_s(z))*cos(pi*z^2/2) - (1/2 - fresnel_c(z))*sin(pi*z^2/2)` (DLMF 7.2.10); it decays like `1/(pi*z)` for large `z` and separates the oscillation of the Fresnel integrals from their slow approach to 1/2.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values; `fresnel_f(0)` is 1/2.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
fresnel_f(0)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
fresnel_f(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.27989340037682287

#% cell
fresnel_f(3.0)
#% output
#= 0.10572078929768558

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The definition:

#% cell
fresnel_f(1.3) - ((0.5 - fresnel_s(1.3))*cos(pi*1.3^2/2) - (0.5 - fresnel_c(1.3))*sin(pi*1.3^2/2))
#% output
#= 0.0

#% text
# The large-argument behaviour `f(z)` close to `1/(pi*z)`:

#% cell
fresnel_f(4.0)*pi*4.0
#% output
#= 0.998828490135902


#% text
# Large arguments (the asymptotic series):

#% cell
[fresnel_f(10.0), fresnel_f(-7.0)]
#% output
#= [0.03183002141511776, 0.954532907453036]

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(fresnel_f(x), x)
#% output
#= -pi*x*fresnel_g(x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `fresnel_g`, `fresnel_s`, `fresnel_c`
